 "sections": [
  {
   "head": "What the graph shows",
   "text": "The number of kinds of sea animals known from each slice of time. Diversity exploded in the Cambrian and Ordovician, held at a plateau through the rest of the Paleozoic, and climbed through the Mesozoic and Cenozoic. Five sharp drops break the climb: the mass extinctions. The worst, at the end of the Permian 252 million years ago, cut known genera by two thirds; their number did not return to its old level until the Jurassic.",
   "sources": [
    "sepkoski2002",
    "raup1982"
   ]
  },
  {
   "head": "How it is known",
   "text": "Jack Sepkoski combed the literature for every fossil marine animal genus, with the oldest and youngest rock in which it is found, some 36,000 genera in all. A genus counts as present in every interval between its first and last appearance. Counting genera rather than species smooths over gaps in the record.",
   "sources": [
    "sepkoski2002",
    "jones2022"
   ]
  },
  {
   "head": "How sure",
   "text": "The mass extinctions stand out in every analysis. The long rise is less certain: younger rocks are more plentiful, better exposed and better studied. When the counts are corrected for how much rock has been sampled, the late Cenozoic comes out less than twice as diverse as the mid-Paleozoic, against two and a half times in the raw counts.",
   "sources": [
    "raup1982",
    "alroy2008"
   ]
  }
 ]
}